title: A loopback security shell, SQLite storage, and a single egress path
---
date: 2026-10-17
---
author: Labyricorn
---
summary: Phase 1 steps one to three build the server shell, versioned SQLite storage, and the only package allowed to make outbound requests.
---
tags: architecture, privacy, sqlite, local-first
---
source_commit: aedf7680f9077d7a397d7b6758ef37747963d021
---
body:

The first three build steps laid the foundation that later features depend on.

**Security shell.** A Go server that binds to loopback, checks the Host header,
Origin, and fetch metadata, issues a session cookie through a one-time launch
code, applies a strict content security policy, and refuses a second instance on
the same data directory with a lock file.

**Storage.** SQLite through a pure-Go driver, with numbered migrations applied
one transaction at a time and tracked by schema version. The server refuses to
open a database from a newer schema and leaves the file untouched. Backups are
written with `VACUUM INTO`, and the credential table is cleared in the copy so a
backup never carries secrets.

**Endpoints, credentials, and egress.** Credentials are protected with the
operating system's per-user facility, with no plaintext fallback on other
platforms. All outbound HTTP lives in one package, and a test scans the source
tree to prove nothing else makes a request. Its dial guard checks the resolved
address on every connection, refuses redirects, rejects cloud metadata
addresses, and scopes each credential to its own endpoint. Public addresses need
an explicit acknowledgement at registration.

A live smoke test found that no stored credential value appeared in the
database, a backup, or the access log. The access log records method, path,
status, and duration only.

Provider detection, model discovery, and the generation pipeline were left for
the next steps.

[Commit aedf768](https://github.com/Labyricorn/Labbonet/commit/aedf7680f9077d7a397d7b6758ef37747963d021)
